# sttp

[Harman's site](https://hrmnjt.dev) — secure thought transfer protocol.
A small personal site built with Hugo: shards, builds, a write-ahead log,
and one readme. System monospace, Gruvbox, and a few useful sketches.
The catalog interleaves content in date order. Article images open in a small,
self-hosted modal viewer; original links still work without JavaScript.

## Run locally

Use Hugo Extended 0.167.0 (the version pinned in CI); the templates need 0.156
or newer. Python 3.11+ is needed only for the repository tests. The site itself needs no Node dependencies or
downloaded fonts. Optional screenshot tooling needs Node 20+ and Chromium.

    hugo server

To see design-only draft WAL entries:

    hugo server --buildDrafts

Never publish a draft-enabled build.

## Build and check

    hugo --panicOnWarning --printPathWarnings
    python3 -m unittest discover -s tests

Hugo generates `public/`; generated output and resource caches are ignored.
Tests build isolated temporary copies, including draft previews and fixtures.
CI also checks generated local links with Lychee.

## Screenshot feedback loop

On your Mac (or supported Ubuntu host), install the development-only tooling:

    npm ci
    npx playwright install chromium

Then capture a normal preview, or opt into design drafts:

    npm run screenshots
    npm run screenshots -- --drafts

The runner starts its own Hugo server on loopback port 1314, shuts everything
down afterwards, and never reuses your existing `hugo server`. In one pass it:

- measures every page in the sitemap, and the 404 page, at desktop (1440×1000)
  and phone (390×844) sizes;
- captures 24 PNGs of the main routes at both sizes;
- writes each main route's accessibility tree as text (`*.aria.yml`): the page
  as a screen reader receives it.

If the port is busy:

    npm run screenshots -- --port 1320

Files are written to `.artifacts/screenshots/normal/` or `drafts/`. These and
`node_modules/` are ignored by Git. Read `report.md` first. Its findings come
from fixed rules over the measurements, so the same site always gives the same
report, and a finding found on many pages is listed once with where it occurs:

| finding      | rule                                                        |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| overflow     | the page scrolls sideways                                   |
| contrast     | text below 4.5:1 against its background (3:1 if large)      |
| heading      | not exactly one `h1`, or a level skipped                    |
| duplicate-id | an `id` used more than once                                 |
| dead-link    | an in-page link to something hidden or missing              |
| target       | a control under 24px with another target within 24px        |
| alt          | an image with no `alt` attribute                            |
| margin       | two things overlapping in the margin column                 |

Broken requests or images, browser errors, and missing capture regions fail the
run too. "Worth knowing" lists what the margin column displaced, without failing.
Each main route also gets its heading outline and tab order. `report.json` has
every number.

Open a PNG when the question is how something looks; no pixel-diff baseline is
required. To skip the pictures:

    npm run screenshots -- --text-only

The runner needs to start a browser and listen on a local port. A sandboxed
coding session may allow neither: run it yourself, or exclude it from the
sandbox, and let the session read the files.

    npm run test:screenshots
    npm run test:images

The image-viewer tests need an installed Chromium browser. They check the deployed
CSP, keyboard/touch dismissal, actual-size scrolling, focus/position restoration,
and no-JavaScript fallback.

## Diagrams

Write a diagram as a `mermaid` code fence, with what it shows as its `alt`:

    ```mermaid {alt="How a request moves through review"}
    flowchart LR
        draft --> review --> decision
    ```

Then draw it and commit the result:

    npm run diagrams

Each fence becomes `assets/images/diagrams/<key>.svg`, where the key is a hash of
the fence's text, and drawings no page uses are removed. Hugo shows the drawing
with its source folded underneath as the text alternative; a fence that has not
been drawn yet stays a code block, and CI fails until it is drawn. Cloudflare Pages needs nothing new, because
the drawings are ordinary committed assets. Colours and type come from the
`--gb-*` and `--font-mono` tokens in `assets/css/main.css`.

    npm run diagrams -- --check    # which fences lack a drawing; needs no browser
    npm run diagrams -- --all      # redraw everything, e.g. after a palette change
    npm run test:diagrams

Drawing needs the same Chromium as the screenshots.

Runner tests require no browser installation; they include a real Hugo server
smoke check when Hugo is available. `npm run screenshots -- --help` lists the
options. Playwright's bundled
browsers do not support Alpine Linux; run captures on your Mac or a supported
Linux host. Browser packages are never part of the deployed site.

## Where things live

- `content/shards/`: dated Markdown writing; URLs retain year/month/day/slug.
- `content/builds/`: build pages, with their own date and repository link.
- `content/wal/`: short dated entries, readable in full on the log page.
- `content/readme/_index.md`: the site's single context document.
- `data/kinds.toml`: the content kinds, in navigation order.
- `assets/`: fingerprinted CSS, image-viewer JavaScript, images, and section sketches.
- `layouts/`: Hugo templates, Markdown hooks, and shortcodes.
- `static/`: favicons and Cloudflare Pages header configuration.
- `scripts/screenshots.mjs`: optional browser capture runner.
- `scripts/diagrams.mjs`: draws `mermaid` fences to `assets/images/diagrams/`.

Use explicit titles and content dates, not automatic Git timestamps. A build
page's date is not the linked shard's date. Keep substantive revisions in
`lastmod`; catalog chronology uses the original `date`. Review draft samples
and personal copy before publishing.

## Design notes

System monospace and Gruvbox dark throughout; `assets/css/main.css` names the
palette (`--gb-*`) and assigns roles from it. Every page shares one frame and
one left rail. The frame is the 70ch prose measure plus a margin column, about
1100px in most monospace fonts. Sizes and breakpoints are in `rem`/`em`, so the
layout follows the reader's font size.

The header is an address: `sttp://hrmnjt.dev/shards/`. Its segments are the
breadcrumb back to the section and home. The stream links sit beside it, with
an asterisk on the current one, the way `git branch` marks it.

Content kinds live in `data/kinds.toml`. Navigation, the home page directory,
catalog tags, article date lines, and JSON-LD all read from it, so a new kind is
one entry there plus its `content/<section>/` directory. A kind is one glyph and
one lowercase name in square brackets, always used together, and three of them
also take a colour:

| kind   | colour          | glyph                         |
| ------ | --------------- | ----------------------------- |
| shard  | Gruvbox purple  | faceted fragment              |
| build  | Gruvbox blue    | cube                          |
| wal    | Gruvbox orange  | ledger page                   |
| readme | neutral         | page with a folded corner     |

The coloured set is closed: any further kind stays neutral. Each glyph is its
section's sketch reduced to an outline and one detail, kept beside the kind in
`data/kinds.toml`. A section's sketch is `assets/images/doodles/<section>.svg`;
it appears on the section's index and in the home page directory. Sketches stay
tan with green accents; only glyphs and labels take a kind's colour.

Four rules hold the look together:

- Square brackets mark text the site adds rather than the author: `[shard]`,
  `[code]`, `[ext]`, `[1]`, `[return]`, `[migrated]`. Green brackets are links,
  dimmer once followed; muted or kind-coloured ones are labels.
- Dashed rules divide the page; dotted lines box content or run vertically.
  Nothing is solid. A rule that opens part of a page carries its name:
  `-- recent ----`, `-- 2026 ----`, `-- footnotes ----`, `-- backlinks ----`.
- Every list of pages is the same ledger row: date, optional `[kind]`, title.
  Indexes break by year.
- Text is 1rem or `--fs-small`. No sizes in between.

Green is reserved for links.

Writing aids:

- `{{< sidenote >}}…{{< /sidenote >}}` is a short aside, lettered so it never
  clashes with numbered footnotes. It sits in the margin column when the article
  is wide enough for one and inline elsewhere. Keep it to one paragraph.
- A Markdown footnote that is one paragraph, cited once, hangs in the same
  margin column beside its `[1]`. Without a margin (phones, print, feeds) it is
  an ordinary footnote under `-- footnotes ----` with a `[return]` link. Longer
  or reused footnotes always stay at the end.
- A page with four or more `##` headings lists them "on this page", in the same
  margin column or folded under the title.
- A Markdown link that leaves the site is tagged `[ext]` by the link render
  hook. Link to other pages with `relref` so they stay internal.
- `{{< img-optimized src="…" alt="…" caption="…" >}}` takes an optional caption.
- A `mermaid` fence becomes a drawing once `npm run diagrams` has run; see
  Diagrams above.
- Tables scroll inside a focusable wrapper. The site sends no inline styles, so
  column alignment is a class.

Shards and builds link to each other with front-matter lists of content paths:

    related_shards = ["/shards/2026-02-01-dev"]
    related_builds = ["/builds/dev"]

Each page's "backlinks" list shows the pages it names and the pages that name
it, newest first. Unresolved, mistyped, or self-referencing entries fail the
build.

## Deployment

Cloudflare Pages builds with plain `hugo` and publishes `public/`; its Hugo
version must match the one above. The base
URL comes from `hugo.toml` (`https://hrmnjt.dev`), not `$CF_PAGES_URL`.
The GitHub Actions workflow checks builds; it does not deploy the site.
Verify headers and canonical/feed URLs on the deployed release.

